{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>ויצעק</b> (and they cried): He says: He will bring them out, for they are at sea and this is their entire request. Rabbi Judah ben Bilam said: it should have been ישב (He sits) in place of יקם (He raises). But what is correct in my eyes: for at first He said ויעמד סערה (a storm arose), and now He raises a storm without wind, then it becomes stillness." "<b>ויחשו גליהם</b> (and their waves were hushed): the waves of the sea, opposite to תרומם גליו (He lifts up its waves). And because waves produce sound, therefore by way of metaphor: 'and they were hushed' for stillness."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 107, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra reads ויעמד רוח סערה from Jonah 1:4, but here paraphrases; the logic is that the first reference involved wind, while in this verse the storm is raised 'without wind' so that it can become still. The citation and argument are compressed as in the original."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:39:04.414452513Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}